faq:
  - q: "What is Amazon's hiring process?"
    a: "Amazon's hiring process for most corporate and technical roles: (1) Application and resume screen. (2) Recruiter phone screen — background, role fit, LP introduction. (3) Phone or virtual screen with a hiring manager. (4) Virtual onsite loop — 4–6 rounds with different interviewers, each assessing multiple Leadership Principles and role-specific skills. (5) Bar raiser round — a cross-functional interviewer who calibrates against the global Amazon standard. (6) Debrief and hiring decision. The bar raiser can veto any hire regardless of other feedback. Amazon is known for a high bar and a relatively fast process — most loops complete within 3–5 weeks."
  - q: "What are Amazon's Leadership Principles and how do they affect hiring?"
    a: "Amazon uses 16 Leadership Principles (LPs) as the core hiring framework — every interview question is designed to assess one or more LPs. The most commonly assessed in loops: Customer Obsession, Ownership, Bias for Action, Dive Deep, Deliver Results, and Are Right, A Lot. You are expected to demonstrate these principles through behavioral examples — not just say you have them. Prepare 2–3 STAR examples per LP that you can adapt to different questions."
  - q: "What is a bar raiser at Amazon?"
    a: "The bar raiser is a specially trained Amazon interviewer who is not part of the hiring team and has no relationship with the hiring manager. Their job is to ensure every hire raises the bar — meaning the candidate is better than 50% of the people currently in that role. The bar raiser has veto power and participates in the debrief with the full panel. A bar raiser 'no hire' overrides positive feedback from the rest of the panel. They typically assess LPs heavily and may ask abstract or challenging questions."
  - q: "How important is the STAR format at Amazon?"
    a: "Critical. Amazon's behavioral interviews are explicitly scored using the STAR format (Situation, Task, Action, Result). Interviewers take structured notes during your answer and look for specific LP evidence. Answers that meander, lack specific actions, or don't include measurable results are scored lower regardless of the underlying quality of the experience. Practice delivering STAR answers in 90–120 seconds before any Amazon interview."
  - q: "Does Amazon hire without a degree?"
    a: "Yes. Amazon hires extensively through its Career Choice program and pathways for roles that do not require degrees. For corporate and technical roles, a degree is common but not a hard requirement — demonstrated skills and experience are weighted heavily, particularly for technical roles. In practice, most software engineering hires at SDE II and above have a degree or equivalent coding bootcamp/self-taught background with strong portfolio evidence."
